---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "How to Calculate Running Pace (and Convert It to Speed) - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "How running pace is calculated from time and distance, how to convert between minutes per kilometre and kilometres per hour, and how to plan a race.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-to-calculate-running-pace.html">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Running</p>
    <h1>How to Calculate Running Pace (and Convert It to Speed)</h1>
    <p class="lead">Pace is the time it takes to cover one kilometre or mile. It is the most useful number for planning a run, because it shows how long a distance will take at a steady effort.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>The formula</h2>
<p>Pace = total time ÷ distance. Ten kilometres in 50 minutes gives 50 ÷ 10 = 5 minutes per kilometre, written 5:00 per km.</p>
<h2>Pace and speed</h2>
<p>Speed is distance ÷ time, usually in kilometres per hour. The same run is 12 km/h. Each pace has one matching speed, so you can convert between them using 60 ÷ pace in minutes.</p>
<h2>Planning a race</h2>
<p>Multiply your target pace by the race distance. A 5:00 per km pace over a 10 km race gives 50 minutes. Use a pace that you can hold for the whole distance, rather than the speed you start with.</p>
<h2>Miles and kilometres</h2>
<p>One mile is 1.609 km. A 5:00 per km pace is about 8:03 per mile, so check which unit your race and watch use.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/running-pace-calculator.html">Running Pace Calculator</a> works out your pace and average speed in km or miles.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
  </div>
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
</Base>
